<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Transportation – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 20px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;">
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Public Transportation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about daily routines. Interactive: Participate in class discussions on everyday topics.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about a daily routine involving transportation (e.g., "Every day, I take the bus to school at 5 a.m., and it's usually crowded." "I walk to school along the main road in my town every morning, and I cross the street at the crosswalk near the market." "I take a boat to school from my village in Portobelo, and the ride takes about 20 minutes.")
Can participate in discussions about transportation preferences (e.g., "I take the bus because it's more affordable, but the metro is the easiest and fastest way to get around the city.")
Can discuss road safety rules with specific examples (e.g., "We have to wear seat belts in the car to stay safe.").</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of public transportation (bus, metro, train, boat) from Panama. Students work in pairs to discuss what they see and briefly share their favorite mode of transportation and why. For example, "I prefer the Metro because it is faster than the bus." or "A boat is sometimes the only way to travel from my village."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using comparatives/superlatives and modal verbs, emphasizing stress in words like "transportation," "convenience," and "passenger." For example, "The Metro is the fastest way to travel in the city, and it's also more convenient than a car." "You should always wear your seatbelt on the bus." The teacher explicitly demonstrates the vowel contrast between /æ/ in "passenger" and /ɑː/ in "port" by saying, "The *passenger* is waiting at the *port*."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'convenient,' is it easy or difficult to use?" (Expected answer: "Easy!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'you should' suggest? An obligation or a recommendation?" (Expected answer: "A recommendation!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic audio dialogue between two Panamanian teenagers discussing their daily commute and transportation choices, including benefits and safety rules. The dialogue features expressions for making suggestions and emphasizing safety, as well as modal verbs, comparatives, and phrasal verbs (e.g., "get on," "get off," "hop on").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret the general idea and specific details of conversations on everyday topics such as public transportation, daily routines, and safety rules.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> Students listen to the dialogue once and answer: "What are the two friends discussing?" and "What is the main topic of their conversation?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and decide if statements related to the dialogue are true or false (e.g., "Maria prefers the bus because it's the fastest way." "They talk about wearing seatbelts in a car.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases from the dialogue to their corresponding meaning or context, specifically focusing on phrasal verbs like "get on," "get off," and "hop on."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using modal verbs for advice and obligation ("should," "have to"), comparatives and superlatives ("faster," "safest"), and phrasal verbs related to transportation ("get on," "take off," "hop on," "get off"). They also practice making suggestions for convenience and emphasizing safety in travel.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students complete sentences orally, choosing the correct modal verb (should/have to) or comparative/superlative adjective. Example: "You ___ wear a seatbelt." (have to) "The Metro is ___ than the bus." (faster) The teacher provides feedback on accuracy and pronunciation, focusing on stress in words like "schedule" and "convenience."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Phrasal Verb Substitution Drill (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher provides a base sentence like "She boards the bus at the station." Students then replace "boards" with "gets on" or "hops on" and say the full sentence aloud. "She gets on the bus at the station." The teacher introduces minimal pairs like /æ/ in "tap" versus /eɪ/ in "tape" and /ɒ/ in "hop" versus /əʊ/ in "hope" through examples like, "I *hope* you can *hop* on the bus before it leaves."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Transportation Preference Chain (Pair Interaction):</b> Students sit in a circle. One student says, "I prefer the bus because it's more affordable." The next student responds, "That's a good point, but I think the train is the most efficient." They must use a comparative/superlative and explain their preference, making sure to use vocabulary like "convenience," "traffic," "fare," or "schedule." Peer correction on grammar and vocabulary use is encouraged.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Fluency focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students will create and perform a short role-play (3-5 minutes) in pairs. One student is a Panamanian teenager explaining their daily commute and transportation routine to a new exchange student (the other student), focusing on the benefits of their chosen method and important safety rules. The exchange student asks questions about convenience, cost, traffic, and safety. Students must include:
        <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Descriptions of their daily routine involving transportation (e.g., "Every morning, I *get on* the Metro at 6 a.m. It's usually crowded, but it's the *fastest* option.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comparisons between different modes of transportation (e.g., "The bus is *more affordable* than a taxi, but sometimes the Metro is *the easiest* way to *avoid traffic*.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Suggestions for convenience (e.g., "You *should* *tap in* your fare card before you *board*.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Emphasis on safety rules (e.g., "You *have to* wait behind the yellow line on the platform." "We *must* always wear *seatbelts* in the car to stay safe on the *highway*.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic element: Incorporate a local protocol (e.g., the local student reminds the exchange student about a specific safety rule in a taxi or on a bus: "Please put your seatbelt on before the taxi trip begins.").</li>
        </ul>
        Students are encouraged to use the target vocabulary (e.g., *passenger*, *journey*, *route*, *traffic light*, *sidewalk*) and practice appropriate stress and vowel contrasts from the lesson.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative Assessment and Reflection)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their role-plays from Stage 4. The teacher uses a checklist to observe the use of target language, while peers provide constructive feedback using a simple rubric on specific speaking aspects.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially stress in multisyllabic words ("transportation," "convenience") and distinction of long/short vowels (e.g., /æ/ vs /ɑː/, /ɒ/ vs /əʊ/).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Consistent use of target vocabulary (e.g., "passenger," "fare," "schedule," "rush hour").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal verbs ("should," "have to") and comparatives/superlatives.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in discussing daily routines, preferences, and safety rules.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective collaboration and interaction with partner.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new phrase or word I used confidently today was..."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I still need to practice more in speaking about transportation is..."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>(Lesson Review and Future Planning)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of our discussion or role-play about transportation did I find most interesting or challenging?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use the new words and phrases about transportation and safety in my daily conversations?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking activities worked well to promote fluency and accuracy in discussing transportation? (e.g., pair work, specific prompts for modal verbs/comparatives)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with pronunciation or integrating all the target language effectively in the role-play? (e.g., provide more sentence starters, explicit modeling of specific phonemes)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary, grammar (modal verbs, comparatives/superlatives), and ideas generated from today's speaking activities to write a paragraph comparing transportation options and discussing their benefits and safety rules. This will transition from oral discussion to structured written expression.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>